WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.000
Detection rules should be tested before broad deployment whenever practical.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.510
Validation helps teams understand whether the rule generates expected signals,

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:11.000
produces excessive noise, or needs adjustment before analysts depend on it.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Testing can include reviewing historical data, using safe lab data,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
comparing against known benign patterns, checking field parsing,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.000
confirming alert enrichment,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.000
and asking analysts whether the output gives them enough context to investigate.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.975
Validation is not only a technical step.

00:00:24.975 --> 00:00:27.995
It also asks whether the detection is operationally useful.

00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:29.975
Does it produce clear output?

00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:30.990
Does it support triage?

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.000
Is the severity reasonable?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:32.995
Are the instructions understandable?

00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:34.975
Is the expected response defined?

00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:37.995
No test guarantees perfect performance, but testing improves confidence.

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:43.065
It helps teams find weak assumptions, missing telemetry, noisy conditions, broken parsing,

00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:46.000
and workflow gaps before those issues create operational friction.
